  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1120 PM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nWhile minimal weather impacts are expected across the forecast area \nthis holiday week, a few disturbances will move through the \nIntermountain West, bringing periodic chances for light snowfall \nacross the mountains. Overall, relatively quiet weather is expected \nthrough the forecast period, with a gradual warming trend beginning \nby mid-week. \n\nThe first disturbance is still on track to move across the region\nlater today, potentially creating some travel headaches along the\nhigh mountain passes and I-70 mountain corridor this afternoon \nand evening. But before it arrives, portions of Lincoln, Elbert, \nand Weld Counties will have to contend with some patchy freezing \nfog this morning, with current observations showing low stratus \nare already beginning to settle in. While a Dense Fog Advisory\ndoesn't seem warranted at this time, conditions will be monitored\nto see how things evolve, but folks should be prepared for some\npatchy fog out there this morning, with the greatest impacts\nexpected along I-70 through Elbert and Lincoln Counties. \n\nWith regards to today's storm system, while the greatest QG \nforcing will remain to our south, a brief period of enhanced QG \nascent is expected as the shortwave tracks across the Rockies. \nForecast soundings show a deeply saturated profile, with the DGZ \nsituated within the layer of strongest lift. Additionally, lapse \nrates approaching 9C/km are expected for a few hours leading up to\nsunset, which could support the development of a few convective \nsnow showers. Given the steep lapse rates and favorable DGZ \nplacement, snowfall rates could briefly exceed what the broader- \nscale forcing might suggest. Thus, while overall snowfall \naccumulations are expected to remain relatively light, briefly \nheavier convective snow showers could create locally hazardous \ntravel conditions, especially across mountain passes and the I-70\nmountain corridor.\n\nQPF has been trended upward to account for this potential and better \nalign with recent guidance. Generally, expecting between 1-3 \ninches of snowfall along the I-70 mountain corridor and \nContinental Divide, although some locally higher amounts cannot be\nruled out where all the ingredients come together. The remainder \nof the mountains should see around an inch or less, with a few \ntenths to a half inch expected for elevations as low as 7,500 \nfeet. PoPs and QPF have also been extended to include the Palmer \nDivide and adjacent plains, where a few tenths to a tenth of an \ninch of snowfall are now included in the latest forecast package. \nTemperatures will remain below seasonal averages today and \nTuesday. \n\nSubsidence will develop Tuesday morning behind the departing system \nas an amplified upper-level ridge shifts eastward across the \nRockies, bringing an end to snow showers and a return to warmer and \ndrier conditions across the forecast area. Afternoon high \ntemperatures will gradually climb through the week, with Thursday's \nholiday weather shaping up to be mild and dry. Highs are expected to \nreach the mid 50s along the urban corridor and mid 40s across the \nmountain valleys. \n\nLooking ahead, ensembles continue to hint at another round of \nmountain snow potentially arriving by Friday morning, while the \nplains are expected to remain dry. Temperatures will trend well \nabove seasonal averages heading into the weekend.",
